<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;general&nbsp;stuff</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>A big plus about this is the &quot;O5 wanting to retire and actually be happy in a life unaffiliated with the Foundation&quot; angle here. It's kinda open for exploration conceptually, and leaves room for some really great emotional punches, parallel to some people's desire to leave work and live a life they consider to be a &quot;happy&quot; one.</p>
<p>Though, I am a tad unsure why the other O5s didn't really arrange like, an advanced surgical procedure to check if she really had a parasitic infection? Hmm. I guess this statement:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>During the procedure, Richard Williams expired, and Dr. Eliot [DATA EXPUNGED].</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Kinda reflects some of the O5s' uncertainty in doing anything about O5-3 since, well, we don't know what would happen if she did undergo such a procedure. But idk, it still stuck out a bit to me.</p>
<p>I kinda liked the interview; it was gut-wrenching to me, especially with how O5-3 (or Dr. Eliot during that time) behaved like a mother to Rick. The ending/twist was a little impactful, though I wasn't sure if it was enough punch; us not seeing O5-3's response to O5-12 saying that he's sorry for not being able to fulfill her wish kinda dampened the big sad a little? Idk, I think it's really a personal preference when it comes to this, but I was kinda hoping for O5-3 to respond back to 12 and say something like &quot;it's ok, and thank you for the effort anyways; Lucy can wait for a few more years, I guess&quot;, maybe?</p>
<p>Hmm. There were definitely some emotional hitters here and there, and the angle taken here is pretty unique, I think, but I wasn't extremely sure if it managed to hit me sufficiently overall. Novote.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;very&nbsp;nitpicky&nbsp;stuff</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-5095 been assumed to be neutralized.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Maybe &quot;SCP-5095 has been assumed&#8230;&quot;?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-5095 is a anomalous</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think &quot;an&quot; fits better here than &quot;a&quot;?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>of the family Taeniidae</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I was thinking swapping the positions of &quot;family&quot; and &quot;Taeniidae&quot; would fit better?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>consuming rational thought</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Hmm. I'm a little iffy on an organism being able to &quot;eat&quot; rational thought here; I was thinking maybe it eats/destroys neurons affiliated with cognitive functions would work slightly better here? Idk.</p>
<p>In the interview, the action cues say &quot;Williams&quot; while Dr. Eliot addressed Richard by &quot;Rick&quot;. I was wondering if going all the way with &quot;Richard&quot; instead of &quot;Williams&quot; would work better? Idk, it personally felt a little troubling to read back to see if I'm seeing the wrong person.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

